1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,871
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:02,921 --> 00:00:06,298
AI for Young Builders, video 6.

3
00:00:06,348 --> 00:00:10,720
How your AI decides: nearest neighbours.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,900
How did your model decide?

5
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:18,584
It used one of the oldest and simplest ideas in AI, called

6
00:00:18,634 --> 00:00:20,280
k nearest neighbours.

7
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:25,042
Imagine every example as a dot on a map.

8
00:00:25,092 --> 00:00:29,000
Here, fruits are placed by sweetness and size.

9
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:36,830
Mangoes gather in one corner, lemons in another, because similar things sit close together.

10
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:39,980
A new fruit arrives.

11
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:45,807
The model measures how far it is from every example, picks the

12
00:00:45,857 --> 00:00:49,002
three closest, and lets them vote.

13
00:00:49,052 --> 00:00:51,446
Two mangoes and one lemon.

14
00:00:51,496 --> 00:00:52,480
Mango wins!

15
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:57,804
The letter k is just how many neighbours get a vote.

16
00:00:57,854 --> 00:01:02,579
With two groups, people pick an odd number like three or five,

17
00:01:02,629 --> 00:01:04,581
so the vote can never tie.

18
00:01:04,631 --> 00:01:09,740
And changing k can change the answer, so it is worth experimenting.

19
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,740
Your drawing model did exactly this.

20
00:01:13,790 --> 00:01:19,144
Each drawing became two hundred and fifty-six pixel numbers, which is a

21
00:01:19,194 --> 00:01:23,482
dot in a space with two hundred and fifty-six directions.

22
00:01:23,532 --> 00:01:27,820
We cannot picture that, but the maths works the same way.

23
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:31,527
Where does the model go wrong?

24
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:36,299
Deep inside a group, every neighbour agrees, so it is easy.

25
00:01:36,349 --> 00:01:42,608
Near the border between groups the neighbours are mixed and the vote is close.

26
00:01:42,658 --> 00:01:46,410
Adding examples near the border helps the most.

27
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:49,188
Let us recap.

28
00:01:49,238 --> 00:01:52,893
Similar examples sit close together.

29
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:55,775
The k nearest examples vote.

30
00:01:55,825 --> 00:02:01,128
Mistakes happen most near the border between groups.

31
00:02:01,178 --> 00:02:04,730
Next: Testing: is your AI any good?
